# A shared struct (§5.4) is built with `new`, and only one is; its fields are read through it
# with `->`, never `.`; and its values have an identity, so they never leave their instance, nor
# does compile time compute one into a top-level const, by itself or as a field's default (§5.1).
# `?->` reads through an optional shared struct only, and a value read through it is a copy, so a
# method taking `&self` isn't called through it (§10.4).

